दुस्सिक्खिअरअणपसिक्खएहि घिट्ठो सि पत्थरे ताव,जा तिलमेत्तं वट्टसि मरगअ का तुज्झ मुल्लकहा	O emerald, A succession of incompetent jewelers Has ground you so long on the stone That you are no bigger than a sesame seed. So what's this talk about being valuable?
जह चिंतेइ परिअणो आसंकइ जह अ तस्स पडिवक्खो,वालेण वि गामणिणंदणेण तह रक्खिआ पल्ली	As his relatives suspected And his enemies feared, The headman's son, Though still very young, Has proved excellent At protecting the village.
गअवहुवेहव्वअरो पुत्तो मे एक्ककंडविणिवाई,तह सोण्हाइ पुलइओ जह कंडवरंडअं वहइ	That daughter-in-law has turned our son Into a trembling wreck: Once he needed just one arrow To make an elephant cow a widow, Now he carries a quiverful.
अप्पाहेइ मरंतो पुत्तं पल्लीवई पअत्तेण,मह णामेण जह तुमं ण लज्जसे तह करेज्जासु	"On his deathbed The headman of the hamlet Earnestly instructs his son: ""Act in such a way As not to be ashamed of my name."""
अणुमरणपत्थिआए पच्चागअजीविए पिअअमम्मि,वेहव्वमंडणं कुलवहूअ सोहग्गअं जाअं	As the good wife prepared To join her beloved on the pyre He came back to life, And her widow's finery Turned to that of married bliss.
महुमच्छिआइ दट्ठं दट्ठूण मुहं पिअस्स सूणोट्ठं,ईसालुई पुलिंदी रुक्खच्छाअं गआ अण्णं	When the Pulinda hunter's wife Saw her husband's lip Swollen by a bee sting, She was stung herself By jealousy And fled To stand in the shade of another tree.
धण्णा वसंति णीसंकमोहणे वहलपत्तलवइम्मि,वाअंदोलणओणविअवेणुगहणे गिरिग्गामे	Lucky are they who live In a mountain village Where one can make love Undisturbed Beneath a leafy hedge Or in deep thickets of reeds That bend and sway in the wind.
तह परिमलिआ गोवेण तेण हत्थं पि जा ण ओल्लेइ,स च्चिअ खडणा एण्हिं पेच्छह कुडदोहिणी जाआ	That froward cow, Which did not even wet the cowherd's hand However much he squeezed her teats— Look how she's now yielding milk By the pail.
धवलो जिअइ तुह कए धवलस्स कए जिअंति गिट्ठीओ,जिअ तंवे अम्ह वि जीविएण गोट्ठं तुमाअत्तं	The bull lives only for you And the heifers only for the bull, So please, dear cow, stay alive For the life of our herd Depends on your life.
अग्घाइ छिवइ चुंवइ ठवेइ हिअअम्मि जणिअरोमंचो,जाआकवोलसरिसं पेच्छह पहिओ महुअपुप्फं	Look how the traveler thrills As he sniffs at the sweetwood flower, Caresses it, kisses it, And presses it against his heart As if it were his wife's pale cheek.
उअ ओल्लिज्जइ मोहं भुअंगकित्तीअ कडअलग्गाइ,ओज्झरधारासद्धालुएण सीसं वणगएण	See how the elephant tries in vain To wet its head beneath a snake's sloughed skin That hangs down from a rocky sill, Mistaking it for a waterfall.
कमलं मुअंत महुअर पिक्ककइत्थाण गंधलोहेण,आलेक्खलड्डुअं पामरो व्व छिविऊण जाणिहिसि	To desert the lotus For the scent of ripe wood-apple— As soon as you touch it, silly bee, You'll find out your mistake, Like the yokel Who reached for a sweetmeat in a painting.
गिज्जंते मंगलगाइआहि वरगोत्तदिण्णअण्णाए,सोउं व णिग्गओ उअह होंतवहुआइ रोमंचो	Look, When the women start singing the wedding songs Goose pimples break out on the bride-to-be As if they too want to hear The bridegroom's name.
मण्णे आअण्णंता आसण्णविवाहमंगलुग्गीअं,तेहि जुआणेहि समं हसंति मं वेअसकुडुंगा	I suspect the reed beds by the river As well as the young men Had a good laugh when they heard The solemn song of blessing On the eve of my wedding.
ण अ दिट्ठिं णेइ मुहं ण अ छिविउं देइ णा लवइ किं पि,तह वि हु किं पि रहस्सं णववहुसंगो पिओ होइ	She does not look at you, Does not allow you to touch her face, Says nothing. Yet, In some mysterious way, Being with a young bride is pure joy.
पुच्छिज्जंती ण भणइ गहिआ पप्फुरइ चुंविआ रुअइ,तुण्हिक्का णववहुआ कआवराहेण उवऊढा	When he asks her a question She does not reply, When he touches her She recoils, When he kisses her She starts to cry, And when he attempts an embrace His young wife's silence Makes him feel like a criminal.
तत्तो च्चिअ होंति कहा विअसंति तहिं तहिं समप्पंति,किं मण्णे माउच्छा एक्कजुआणो इमो गामो	The girl's thick, fragrant hair Is like a column of smoke rising from the fire of love, Like a bunch of peacock's tail-feathers Waved by the conjuror to distract his audience, Like the victory banner of youth.
जाइ वअणाइ अम्हे वि जंपिमो ताइ जंपइ जणो वि,ताइं चिअ तेण पजंपिआइ हिअअं सुहावेंति	Who is not captivated by a woman's breasts, That, like a good poem, Are a pleasure to grasp, Are weighty, compressed, and nicely ornamented?
सव्वाअरेण मग्गह पिअं जणं जइ सुहेण वो कज्जं,जं जस्स हिअअदइअं तं ण सुहं जं तहिं ण त्थि	If you want to be happy Take care in choosing a lover, For what is dear to you Does not bring happiness Unless you are dear to it.
दीसंतो दिट्ठिसुहो चिंतिज्जंत मणवल्लहो अत्ता,उल्लावंत सुइसुहो पिओ जणो णिच्च रमणिज्जो	When one sees him He gives satisfaction to the eyes, When one thinks of him He gives satisfaction to the mind, When he speaks He gives satisfaction to the ears: Dear mother-in-law, My husband is always delightful.
ठाणब्भट्ठा परिगलिअपीणआ उण्णईअ परिचत्ता,अम्हे उण ठेरिपओहर व्व उअरे च्चिअ णिसन्णा	Fallen from our former position, Our abundance wasted away, No longer prominent, We stare at our navels Like an old woman's breasts.
पच्चूसागअ रंजिअदेह पिआलोअ लोअणाणंद,अण्णत्तखविअसव्वरि णहभूसण दिणवइ णमो दे	What a feast for the eyes, When you return at dawn Red all over Having spent the night elsewhere. Ornament of the sky, Lord of the day, We bow before you humbly.
विवरीअसुरअलेहड पुच्छसि मह कीस गब्भसंभूइं,ओअत्ते कुंभमुहे जललवकणिआ वि किं ठाइ	You who like to make love upside down, How can you ask if I am pregnant? Is there a single drop of water left In a jug turned upside down?
अच्चासण्णविवाहे समं जसोआइ तरुणगोवीहिं,वड्ढंते महुमहणे संवंधा णिण्हुविज्जंति	When Kṛṣṇa came of age And the time of his marriage drew near The young cowgirls tried to conceal their family ties With his mother Yaśodā.
दूरंतरिए वि पिए कह वि णिअत्ताइ मज्झ णअणाइ,हिअअं उण तेण समं अज्ज वि अणिवारिअं भमइ	When my lover left for distant lands My eyes found their way back home, But my heart is still beside him Going wherever he goes.
तस्स कहाकंतइए सद्दाअण्णणसमोसरिअकोवे,समुहालोअणकंपिरि उवऊढा किं पवज्जिहिसि	At the mention of his name you bristle; At the sound of his voice your anger melts; Seeing him face to face, you tremble. What will you do when you embrace?
भरणमिअणीलसाहग्गखलिअचलणद्धविहुअवक्खउडा,तरुसिहरेसु विहंगा कह कह वि लहंति संठाणं	With great difficulty Birds find a place to rest In the treetops, Fluttering their wings And shifting their feet At the tips of the dark branches That bend beneath their weight.
